{
  "id": "Compound::DB00983 - Disease::DOID:3083",
  "verbalization": "Formoterol, a member of the adrenergic beta-2 agonists, is implicated in the treatment of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) through its role in activating beta-2 receptors, which can relax airway smooth muscle and improve airflow. This mechanism is supported by the inclusion of Formoterol in the adrenergic beta-2 agonists category and its association with COPD in multiple pathways. The shared lowest common ancestor between Formoterol and Arformoterol, as well as between Formoterol and Salbutamol, underscores the broader class of beta-2 agonists that may share similar therapeutic effects. While the direct treatment link between Formoterol and COPD is not explicitly stated, the indirect pathways through beta-2 receptor activation and the broader class of adrenergic agonists provide a plausible mechanism for its potential use in COPD management. The evidence suggests that Formoterol may exert a beneficial effect on COPD through its adrenergic agonist properties, though further clinical validation is needed to establish its efficacy in this context.",
